      "kind": "seed",
      "text": "The change log starts here — the two-party preferred estimate is 52.3 (Labor v One Nation, implied flows). From today, every movement it makes and every statistical finding that comes or goes is recorded here."
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-12",
      "label": "12 Oct 2026",
      "kind": "poll",
      "text": "Roy Morgan (fieldwork 28 Sep–11 Oct, n=2,655) added — the two-party preferred estimate moved 52.3 → 52.4 (Labor v One Nation, implied flows).",
      "url": "https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10366-federal-voting-intention-october-12-2026"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-12",
      "label": "12 Oct 2026",
      "kind": "finding",
      "text": "NSW voters (by state) no longer break significantly toward others, relative to all voters."
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-12",
      "label": "12 Oct 2026",
      "kind": "seed",
      "text": "From today the log also records the primary votes, the leaders' ratings, national direction and the house effect of each pollster that publishes, every measure whose month-on-month move becomes, or stops being, statistically significant, and changes in which party leads significantly as the best on each issue. Primary votes now: Labor 27.5, the Coalition 21.6, One Nation 26.1, the Greens 13.4, others 11.4. Leader ratings now: Albanese's net approval −21.6, Albanese's net favourability −20.7, Taylor's net approval −6.9, Taylor's net favourability −4.7, Hanson's net approval −2.6, Hanson's net favourability −5.6. National direction now: right track 27.1, wrong track 62.2 (net −35.1). Leading significantly as the best party now: Labor on climate change; One Nation on crime; the Coalition on economic management; Labor on health; One Nation on immigration. Moving significantly now: the share naming economic management a top-three issue (up); Labor's share as the best party on housing (down)."
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-12",
